  🔴 Critical Blockers (must fix before launch)                                                                                                                                                           
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  ---
  1. YouTube Shorts — Local Filesystem Won't Work in Production      --Done                                                                                                                                      
                                                               
  Right now clips are saved to backend/clips_data/ on the local machine. On any cloud host (Vercel, Railway, Render, Fly.io), the filesystem is ephemeral — files disappear on every redeploy.
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  Options:
  - Cloudinary (simplest — free 25GB, video support, already has CLOUDINARY_* keys in config) — upload the MP4 after yt-dlp clips it, store the Cloudinary URL instead                                    
  - Cloudflare R2 — cheap (~$0.015/GB), S3-compatible, no egress fees                                                                                                                                     
  - AWS S3 — industry standard, more setup                           
  - Persistent volume on Railway/Render/Fly — if you keep the backend on a single server with a mounted disk, local filesystem actually works fine in prod                                                
                                                                                                                                                          
  Recommendation: Use Cloudinary (it's already half-wired in your config) or Railway with a persistent volume (zero code changes needed).                                                                 
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  ---                                                                                                                                                                                                     
  2. Supabase RLS is Disabled                                                                                                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  Every user can theoretically read/write every other user's data if they call the DB directly. The service role key bypasses RLS anyway from the backend, but if any key leaks, it's a problem. More
  importantly, it's not production-grade.                                                                                                                                                                 
                                                            
  What needs to happen:                                                                                                                                                                                   
  - Re-enable RLS on content_items, agent_runs, notifications, connected_accounts
  - Add policies: USING (user_id = auth.uid()) — but since you use Clerk (not Supabase Auth), auth.uid() won't match. You have two options:                                                               
    - Option A (simpler): Keep RLS disabled, keep the service role key only on the backend, and rely on your API layer for access control — this is acceptable if the key never touches the frontend
    - Option B (proper): Use JWT claims — configure Supabase to trust Clerk JWTs, then auth.uid() will resolve to the Clerk user ID                                                                       
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  Recommendation: Option A for now — your backend already enforces user_id on every query. Add it to the launch backlog.                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  ---                                                                                                                                                                                                     
